  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The fire-core in Keeper Vex's flipper had gone the color of old blood. She set it on the stone between them and did not look at it again.
    
    "Three keepers before me," she said. "The first drowned herself. The second walked into the deep and went silent. I'm the third, and my flippers shake, Sivruta~. They shake when I hold it and they shake when I set it down."
    
    Sivruta~ said nothing. The Glow-Fissure's light caught the chain at their throat—blackened fire-core links, the Mask hanging like a second sternum beneath ash-grey wrappings. They did not touch it. Not while someone was watching.
    
    Vex pushed the grimoire across the stone. ☽'☽'numu' Witness Not a blade—the kind of misdirection Nightshelf keepers favored when outsiders asked questions worth killing over. Small enough to fit in a flipper. Heavy as grief. The volcanic glass covers were layered with ancestor-bone carved into sigils that made the eyes slide sideways, and the spine was fractured clean—a break from the night the Weal' Clique~ carried it through the Bra∴conflictu rather than let it fall. No one had repaired it. No one would.
    
    "The ink reveals itself only in perfect darkness," Vex said. "You understand what that means."
    
    Sivruta~ understood. It meant extinguishing every fire-core to read a single page. It meant choosing knowledge over warmth in a place where warmth was measured in days of life remaining.
    
    "The bells are at the Bleeder," Vex continued. "Where Grandfather Blood set them down and walked into the vents. They're still warm. Still pulsing. The Cell claims ownership—they claim everything—but they lost Ravium and they know it, and knowing it has made them cruel in the specific way that broken guilds become cruel." She paused. "Otrolal would have gone already. Otrolal wouldn't have asked."
    
    There it was. The name that followed Sivruta~ through every tunnel, every hall, every whispered conversation that stopped when they entered. Otrolal the Watchful, who died in the Thron∴mbra Fracta holding the dark so others could keep their fires. The greater hero. The one the colony remembered.
    
    Sivruta~ picked up the grimoire. The fractured spine pressed against their flipper like a wound that had chosen to stay open.
    
    "Otrolal is dead," Sivruta~ said. "I'm not."
    
    They left the Glow-Fissure with three fire-cores, the grimoire, and the Mask's weight against their chest. Behind them, Vex's dying fire-core guttered to nothing, and the dark came in.
    
    ---
    
    The contested tunnels below the fourth depth had no master. This was the kind of freedom that killed.
    
    Sivruta~ followed courier-marks scratched into the ice—faint glowing lines that only showed in the peripheral dark, the ink designed to be seen by those who knew not to look directly. The marks led through passages where cold-burn discoloration crept along the walls in patterns too regular to be natural, where whisper-echoes bounced from breach-points that had never been sealed. Corruption thresholds. The ice here remembered things that hadn't happened yet.
    
    They smelled The Cell before they saw them. Fire-core oil, the specific blend the owl-sworn used to keep their amulets polished—a scent that was part maintenance, part ritual, part refusal to admit what they'd become.
    
    Three of them stepped from a side passage. Two carried fire-core hammers with cracked heads; the third, older, broader, with the deliberate stillness of someone who'd learned patience from violence, carried nothing visible. That made them the dangerous one.
    
    "Void-mage." The older penguin's voice carried the flat courtesy of a toll-collector. "These routes belong to The Cell."
    
    "These routes belonged to The Cell," Sivruta~ said. "Before Ravium. Before the Midnight Claws walked in while you were still bleeding."
    
    Silence. The kind that preceded either negotiation or murder.
    
    "We don't joke about Ravium," said one of the hammer-carriers.
    
    "I'm not joking. I'm establishing what you have and what you've lost, so we can discuss what I'm willing to pay for passage to the Bleeder."
    
    The older penguin—Mercator ☽'Umbrlecnencavae, Sivruta~ guessed, from the way the others deferred without looking—tilted their head. "The Bleeder. And what waits at the Bleeder that a void-mage wrapped in ash wants badly enough to walk our dark?"
    
    "Something you claim to own but haven't touched. Something that makes flippers tremble."
    
    The Mercator's eyes changed. Not surprise; recognition. "The Twilight-bells are owed to us. For what we lost when Ravium fell. For what we spent holding the routes when every other guild ran. That instrument is payment, void-mage. Not treasure."
    
    Sivruta~ set one fire-core on the stone between them. In the current shortage, it was weeks of warmth. A small fortune. A piece of survival surrendered.
    
    "Payment for passage," Sivruta~ said. "Not for the bells. You can't sell what you can't hold."
    
    The Mercator looked at the fire-core. Looked at Sivruta~. Picked it up with the careful reverence of someone who remembered when these were common and understood they never would be again.
    
    "The lower tunnels are corrupted past the sixth junction," the Mercator said. "Courier-marks end at the bone-token wall. After that, you're reading ice-memory or you're dead." A pause. "The ones who carried it longest carried it unwillingly. Remember that, when you find what you're looking for."
    
    They stepped aside. Sivruta~ passed through with two fire-cores remaining, one lighter, the Mask cold against their chest.
    
    ---
    
    Past the bone-token wall, the dark thickened.
    
    Not metaphor. The ice here absorbed light; fire-core radiance that should have reached three body-lengths barely managed one. Sivruta~'s remaining cores burned visibly faster, their warmth radius shrinking with each hundred steps deeper. The walls bore names carved by miners who didn't come back up—some in careful script, some gouged in panic, the letters getting larger as if the carvers needed to prove they'd existed. Below the names, deeper, older: symbols that predated the colony, written in something that might be ash or might be blood.
    
    Deeper still; darker still; warmer still.
    
    The warmth was wrong. It seeped from below, from the geothermal veins that fed the Bleeder, and it carried the smell of iron and sulfur and the sweet rot of things that had learned to live in absolute dark. Sivruta~ stopped at a ledge where the passage widened into nothing, and knew what the grimoire demanded.
    
    They set ☽'☽'numu' Witness on the stone. Looked at their two remaining fire-cores. Looked at the dark.
    
    *The ink reveals itself only in perfect darkness.*
    
    Sivruta~ killed the first core. Wrapped it in insulating cloth, sealed it, felt the warmth-radius halve. The passage shrank around them—walls closer, ceiling lower, the dark pressing in with physical weight.
    
    Killed the second.
    
    Absolute black. The kind that had weight and texture and breath. The kind that made the ice-memory stir, made whisper-echoes coalesce into almost-words, made the corruption in the walls pulse like a living thing.
    
    The grimoire's pages began to glow.
    
    Luminous ink in the perfect dark—pale blue-green, the color of bioluminescence in water that shouldn't support life. The sigils on the ancestor-bone covers burned with cold light, and the fractured spine leaked radiance like a wound that refused to close. Sivruta~ read with flippers pressed flat against the volcanic glass, feeling the warmth that shouldn't be there, the warmth of a book that remembered being carried through fire.
    
    The pages showed a map. Not of tunnels—of thresholds. Each corruption point between here and the Bleeder marked with a sigil that meant *passage* and another that meant *price*. And at the center, where the Bleeder breathed its poison-and-warmth cycle, a drawing of seven obsidian chimes in a frame of shadow-steel and ancestor bone.
    
    Beneath the drawing, in ink that pulsed with the rhythm of no heart: *What rings between fire and ice cannot be owned. Only carried. Only borne.*
    
    The cold was killing them. Not metaphor; literal. Without fire-core warmth in the deep tunnels, hypothermia came in minutes. Sivruta~ memorized what they could—three thresholds, the turning at the sulfur-seep, the passage that opened when you stopped forcing it—and relit the cores with shaking flippers. The ink died the moment light returned. The grimoire went dark and heavy and silent, a stone book with nothing to say to anyone who insisted on surviving.
    
    Two fire-cores. Both diminished. The warmth that came back was smaller than the warmth that had left.
    
    ---
    
    The third threshold was where they found Otrolal.
    
    Not the body. The body had been recovered from the Thron∴mbra Fracta, given proper rites, the ancestor-bone clasp returned to the family. What waited in the thin-veil territory below the sixth depth was something else: a transparent figure walking the same passages, charcoal wraps layered over leather, burn-scars running the length of a left flipper—waxy, stiff, old fire damage that had followed them past death.
    
    Sivruta~ stopped. The Mask went cold against their chest. Cold and then colder and then a kind of absence of temperature that meant the veil between living and memory had worn to nothing.
    
    Otrolal stood at the passage's branching, where the courier-marks ended and the corruption began in earnest. They looked at Sivruta~ the way they'd always looked at Sivruta~—with the patient expectation of someone who saw the knife but wanted to see if the hand would learn to use it.
    
    They didn't speak. The dead didn't, in the thin-veil spaces. They communicated through absence; through what they didn't say; through the shape of the quiet.
    
    Otrolal turned and walked deeper.
    
    *The Walking.* The thought hit like ice-water. This was how it happened—the ones who'd lost too much, who followed something into the dark and didn't come back. A compulsion rather than a decision. Some were found frozen with peaceful expressions. Others vanished entirely.
    
    Sivruta~ took one step after Otrolal. Then stopped.
    
    The passage Otrolal walked toward was not the passage the grimoire had shown. It was deeper; it was warmer; it was the kind of dark that promised rest to those too tired to keep carrying their fire. And Otrolal—brilliant, relentless, mythic Otrolal—had died in that dark. Not in glory. In the same tunnels they'd always haunted, holding the line because no one else would, and the dark had taken them the way it took everything.
    
    "I'm not following you," Sivruta~ said.
    
    The transparent figure paused. Turned. The burn-scars caught no light because there was no light to catch; they simply existed as texture in the absence.
    
    "I carried your shadow for years," Sivruta~ said. "The lesser hero with the sharper knife. That was enough, when you were alive. It's not enough to die for."
    
    Otrolal looked at them. Through them. Into the place where the Mask hung and the ash-grey wrappings hid the tools no one named. Then the Watchful turned away—not into the deep passage but aside, into the wall, into the ice-memory where the dead lived between the living's visits. The passage they'd guarded opened. Not because Sivruta~ had forced it.
    
    Because they'd stopped following.
    
    On the stone where Otrolal had stood: an ancestor-bone clasp. Their mother's, though they'd never said so. Sivruta~ picked it up. Weighed it. Set it in the offering-niche beside the names of miners who hadn't come back.
    
    The passage opened further, and the smell of iron and sulfur and something ancient rose from below like breath.
    
    ---
    
    ☽'moth Silvesiris~ earned its name.
    
    The cavern opened like a wound in the Berg's belly—vast, steaming, alive with the wrong kind of warmth. The geothermal spring pulsed at its center, bioluminescent green shifting to murky gold and back, fed by thermal vents where blind fish swarmed in clouds of translucent bodies. Steam columns rose and froze before they reached the ceiling, leaving frost-flowers the size of a penguin's head along the stone above, crystalline and dripping. The walls bore Magister ☽'Naevaeum~'s forge-cuts, still weeping heat; bore three generations of names and bone-token offerings; bore, in the deepest recesses where all light failed, symbols older than the colony, older than the ice, written by presences that watched from the compressed deep with patient, ancient attention.
    
    Sivruta~ felt them. Not at the edge of perception—close, direct, a weight of regard that pressed against the skull like deep-water pressure. The watchers. Real, the Nightshelf claimed. The Aurora said belief made them so. Down here the distinction didn't matter.
    
    The Twilight-bells sat on a natural shelf of volcanic stone above the spring, where Grandfather Blood had set them down and walked into the vents without explanation. The steam had not corroded them. The corruption had not touched them. They simply waited, warm, pulsing with captured aurora-light that had no business existing this deep in the dark.
    
    Seven obsidian chimes. Each one hollow, the volcanic glass grain visible under the green bioluminescence—striated, ancient, worked by tools or forces that left no mark. Suspended in a frame of shadow-steel and ancestor bone so old the bone had gone translucent, the marrow channels visible like frozen rivers. The largest bell bore a deep notch—Krathund Gnurl's teeth, from when the orca-sworn had carried it into the sea and brought it back changed. Two others showed white stress-fractures from the Cav∴lapsum, the collapse that killed forty miners and the bells with them, except the bells came back and the miners didn't.
    
    The frame was warm. Always warm. No source, no fire, no core—only the aurora trapped within, pulsing in rhythm with no heart, light that shouldn't exist fused with volcanic glass that shouldn't hold it. What rings between fire and ice. A contradiction made physical. A transgression made beautiful.
    
    Sivruta~ reached for them and their flippers trembled.
    
    Not from cold; not from fear. From the frequency. The bells vibrated at a pitch below sound, below hearing, a resonance that traveled through ice and flesh and bone and made old wounds ache. The Mask at Sivruta~'s throat hummed in answer—one artifact recognizing another, one claiming recognizing the shape of what claiming cost.
    
    They tried to lift the frame. It didn't move. Not heavy—*anchored*. To the stone, or the ice beneath the stone, or something that required understanding before strength.
    
    Sivruta~ knelt. Put their flippers on the cavern floor. Felt the deep-ice beneath volcanic rock, the watchers pressing close, the pulse of the spring like a question that had outlived everyone who'd tried to answer it with force.
    
    "What the fire costs," they said to the darkness. Not a question. A recognition.
    
    The vibration shifted. Something in the bells responded—not to the meaning but to the *knowing*. The Nightshelf structured initiation through multiple chambers, each darker than the last, each demanding you carry something forward that weighed more than it should. This was that architecture, but written by the Berg itself.
    
    "Deeper than necessary," Sivruta~ continued. Each phrase pulled from memory of Otrolal's teaching, from the way the Watchful had moved through shadow like water, from the understanding that some knowledge costs more than it illuminates.
    
    "Silence that answers."
    
    The frame loosened. Not from the stone—from whatever had bound it *to* the stone. The watchers pressed close and weighed the seeker and found—
    
    What? Sivruta~ didn't know. The watchers didn't speak. They simply receded, patient, as if they'd waited centuries for this and would wait centuries more for what came next.
    
    Sivruta~ lifted the Twilight-bells. The weight was impossible and precise: not heavy, not light, but *present* in a way that ordinary objects were not. The aurora-light within the glass shifted at their touch, cycling through colors that had no names in the Nightshelf tongue—colors from the surface, from the sky, from the world above that Sivruta~ had not seen in years. The bells did not ring. They vibrated, and the stone of the cavern vibrated with them, and the spring's bioluminescence flared bright enough to cast shadows, and every scar on Sivruta~'s body remembered why it happened.
    
    ---
    
    The bells pulsed against Sivruta~'s chest where the Mask had hung. They'd moved the chain aside without thinking; the Twilight-bells had settled into the space like they'd been shaped for it. The warmth spread through the ash-grey bindings, through the bone-work tools in the inward-facing pockets, through the scars that mapped a life spent in the deep dark.
    
    Sivruta~ stood at the edge of the spring and felt the corruption cycle turn—clear water going murky, the sweet rot rising, the Bleeder beginning its slow exhale of poison. In a few hours this chamber would be toxic. In a few weeks it would be clean again. The bells had survived every cycle. So had the watchers. So had the ice-memory carved into walls by hands that predated everything Sivruta~ understood.
    
    *Take what calls to you, but remember what answers.*
    
    The Twilight-bells called. They called with a voice below sound, a vibration that promised power in a time when fire-cores failed and aurora-light dimmed and every faction in the Nightshelf would kill for what Sivruta~ held. The Cell would claim them. The Communion would sanctify them. The Weal' Clique~ would seal them away. And whoever carried them would be changed—Grandfather Blood walking into the vents, the three keepers of the grimoire drowning or going silent or shaking, every unwilling carrier transformed by the weight of holding fire and ice together in the same frame.
    
    Sivruta~ knew the cost of being claimed by power. The Mask had taught that. The Mask hung cold and patient at their throat, and the bells hung warm and patient at their chest, and between the two artifacts Sivruta~ felt the specific vertigo of someone standing on a line they'd been walking toward their whole life.
    
    They could set the bells down. Walk back up through the thresholds, past the corruption, past the bone-token wall and the Cell's toll-routes and the Glow-Fissure where Vex waited with shaking flippers. Leave the treasure for the next seeker, the next unwilling carrier, the next penguin foolish enough to reach for something that changed everyone who touched it.
    
    They could keep walking. Deeper. Into the vents where Grandfather Blood had gone, where Otrolal's ghost had tried to lead them, where the Walking promised rest to those too tired to carry anything at all.
    
    Or they could carry it. Not own it—the grimoire had been clear, and the watchers had been clear in their ancient silence, and every century of the bells' history sang the same lesson. The worthy seeker was not the one who wanted it most. The worthy seeker was the one who understood why it should not be wanted, and picked it up anyway, and walked back toward the living.
    
    Sivruta~ wrapped the Twilight-bells in the ash-grey binding from their left flipper, exposing the bone-work tools beneath—the tools they'd never named, the ones that faced inward, the evidence of a competence bought with something essential. They settled the wrapped bells against their back. The vibration traveled through their spine, their ribs, their sternum, finding resonance with the Mask's chain like two notes of a chord that needed a third.
    
    Deeper still; darker still; warmer still. But Sivruta~ turned toward the surface.
    
    Two diminished fire-cores. A grimoire that only spoke in the dark. A dead mentor's passage standing open behind them. The long climb back through contested territory where every faction would smell what they carried and come asking with hammers and hunger and competing claims.
    
    Sivruta~ touched the Mask at their throat. Then the bells at their back. Two artifacts, two choosings, two weights that no one in the Glow-Fissure would envy once they understood the cost.
    
    The spring behind them cycled toward poison. The frost-flowers dripped. Somewhere in the deep ice, the watchers settled back into their ancient patience, and the names of miners who never returned glowed faintly in the dark, and Sivruta~ climbed.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771584562435_0", anchor: "the spine was fractured clean—a break from the night the Weal' Clique~ carried it through the Bra∴conflictu rather than let it fall. No one had repaired it. No one would.", text: "The artifact record states the fracture sealed itself after the Witness cleansed the corruption at Wane☽ Oncea. Either the sealing was partial and this chronicler saw the residual break, or it reopened — artifacts of this class have precedent for that. But \"No one would\" is a strange certainty to hold about an object that demonstrably heals. Someone is wrong. Possibly everyone.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584562435_1", anchor: "faint glowing lines that only showed in the peripheral dark", text: "Courier-ink. The Courier Corps marks routes through contested territory with ink visible in both light and dark — but this variant, designed for peripheral vision only, is a Nightshelf deep-tunnel adaptation. That the Corps maintained navigation infrastructure this far below the fourth depth, where their inviolability under the Flipper Accord could not conceivably be enforced, says more about the couriers than any official tribute I have read.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_2", anchor: "Krathund Gnurl's teeth, from when the orca-sworn had carried it into the sea and brought it back changed", text: "\"Orca-sworn\" implies organized collaboration — penguins or orcas operating under pact. This is not the same as the orca war-pods that conduct blood-rituals and drive wedge-attacks through shore ice. The distinction matters enormously. If the bells were carried into the sea by treaty and returned, that suggests a level of orca-penguin cooperation the standard histories refuse to accommodate. If this was a raid reframed as transaction, the chronicler has done the reader a disservice. One sentence should not carry this much unsourced weight.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_3", anchor: "The ice here remembered things that hadn't happened yet.", text: "The chronicler treats this as atmosphere. It is not. I have documented forward-impressions in three Foundation Depths surveys and been told each time my methodology was flawed. The conditions described here — corruption thresholds active at densities the pre-Incursion records do not support, whisper-echoes from breach-points that canonical timelines place as unsealed only after Incursion-era destabilization — belong to a later period. If the deep ice was showing Sivruta~ the aftermath of what had not yet begun, the implications are ones I cannot professionally endorse and cannot professionally dismiss. The ice has opinions. I have said this before. No one listens.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584562435_4", anchor: "they lost Ravium and they know it, and knowing it has made them cruel in the specific way that broken guilds become cruel", text: "The Mercator accepting a single fire-core for passage rather than pressing a territorial claim is the arithmetic of an organization that can no longer afford principles. Aligns precisely with what I documented in the Cell entry. Still dangerous. Still meeting in rotating caverns. Still claiming everything.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_5", anchor: "the collapse that killed forty miners", text: "Thirty-seven confirmed dead. The Cav∴lapsum records list thirty-seven. Forty is the number that entered memory. It always is.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_6", anchor: "The first drowned herself. The second walked into the deep and went silent.", text: "The second keeper's fate reads as the Walking. Vex does not name it — either from discretion or the particular Nightshelf reluctance to speak the word, as if naming it extends the invitation.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_7", anchor: "burn-scars running the length of a left flipper", text: "Left flipper. Different source, same location. I am aware this is not a scholarly observation. The Whisper Galleries left marks of their own on me, and the formal apology sits unopened not because I haven't forgiven the Nightshelf but because reading it would require translating what happened into someone else's language for what it cost. Otrolal's scars followed them past death. Some do.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_8", anchor: "symbols older than the colony, older than the ice, written by presences that watched from the compressed deep with patient, ancient attention", text: "This is the most direct account of the pre-penguin presences I have encountered outside the Foundation Depths themselves. The chronicler treats the watchers as set-dressing — atmospheric pressure for the treasure-hunt narrative. They are not set-dressing. The deep archive contains impressions from these entities that I have catalogued for years and cannot explain to anyone's satisfaction, including my own. That they press close to Sivruta~, assess them by unknown criteria, and recede in apparent satisfaction suggests something operating on a logic that predates every framework we possess. The Nightshelf claim they are real. Down here, alone in my archive at this hour, I do not have the professional distance to disagree.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584562435_9", anchor: "Magister ☽'Naevaeum~'s forge-cuts", text: "Attribution without dating. ☽'Naevaeum~'s active period spans nearly the entire Clever Ice Age. Which decade determines whether the Bleeder was mapped before or after the Cell established toll-routes — a sequence that matters for ownership claims the chronicle treats as settled.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584562435_10", anchor: "Somewhere in the deep ice, the watchers settled back into their ancient patience", text: "The chronicle's timeframe extends into the Orca Incursion. War-pods are striking colonies. Not one rumor reaches these tunnels. Not one courier fails to arrive. Not one whisper-channel carries what every Nightshelf operative above the fourth depth already knew. The deep tunnels create natural information blackouts — I accept this. But the silence here is so complete it reads less like isolation than like a chronicle that refused to look up.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
